entations they have.", "did": "He recommends staying uncertain about LLM world models and distinguishing in-context from out-of-context reasoning when judging their human-likeness.", "alternatives_named": ["assuming LLMs have human-like representations because they behave human-like on many prompts"], "stated_reason": "Models can be human-like in in-context reasoning but not in out-of-context reasoning, as the Reversal Curse shows."}, {"key": "d10", "type": "advice", "problem": "How others can test the Reversal Curse themselves on chat models without confounding the result.", "did": "He recommends first verifying the model knows the forward fact, then asking the reverse question in a separate dialog that never contains the child's name.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "If the name appears in the dialog, the model can answer from context rather than from what it learned in training."}, {"key": "d11", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether humans also show a Reversal Curse, which bears on how to read the LLM result.", "did": "He states his guess that humans learn one-directionally but labels it highly speculative and supports it with everyday observations.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "People cannot easily recite poems backwards or understand reversed language."}, {"key": "d12", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether model behavior on out-of-context generalization tasks is explained by near-Bayesian-optimal reasoning.", "did": "He cites evidence against models being close to Bayesian optimality: realized descriptions have a smaller effect on unrealized descriptions than a Bayesian account would predict.", "alternatives_named": ["Models behave close to Bayesian optimal reasoning"], "stated_reason": "The ground truth for optimal reasoning is hard to define, and the observed effects are smaller than a Bayesian account predicts."}, {"key": "d13", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether models' success at predicting the training set explains their ability to verbalize latent structure.", "did": "He notes that in-distribution learning is much more reliable than generalizing to verbalization evaluations, so predicting the training set does not automatically produce verbalization.", "alternatives_named": ["Verbalization comes for free from learning to predict the training set"], "stated_reason": "Models learn in-distribution far more reliably than they generalize to the verbalization evaluations."}, {"key": "d14", "type": "advice", "problem": "How strongly to state conclusions about how out-of-context reasoning scales with model size.", "did": "He declines to draw firm scaling conclusions from a single GPT-3.5 to GPT-4 improvement.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "There is not enough information to support firm conclusions about scaling."}, {"key": "d15", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to study introspection when ground truth about internal states is expensive, noisy or contested.", "did": "He suggests evaluating models on very large, diverse datasets where ground truth exists, and averaging over many questions where it is noisier, to gather evidence.", "alternatives_named": ["Give up on introspection science where ground truth is unavailable"], "stated_reason": "Findings from datasets with ground truth may help in cases where ground truth is noisier and more expensive."}, {"key": "d16", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether a model predicting its own hypothetical behavior is just rephrasing the question, which would make the self-prediction advantage trivial.", "did": "He argues it is not mere rephrasing, because models without finetuning do poorly at this task and a model is not always better at predicting itself than other models are.", "alternatives_named": ["Treat hypothetical self-prediction as equivalent to rephrasing the object-level question"], "stated_reason": "Models perform poorly on hypotheticals without finetuning, and GPT-3.5 predicts itself worse than Llama-70B predicts GPT-3.5."}, {"key": "d17", "type": "advice", "problem": "How to interpret a negative or weak introspection result in a model that failed to show it.", "did": "He cautions that failure to elicit a capability in a weaker model may reflect scale and elicitation technique, so a null result should not be taken as showing models cannot do it.", "alternatives_named": ["Conclude LLMs cannot do more introspective-like behavior than shown"], "stated_reason": "They failed to elicit introspection in GPT-3.5, so a project run a year earlier would have found nothing introspective."}, {"key": "d18", "type": "decision", "problem": "Which sense of introspection to prioritize when designing tests for LLM introspection.", "did": "He prioritizes introspection on current mental states, rather than on behavioral patterns another model could learn from the same data.", "alternatives_named": ["Introspection about patterns in behavior such as personality traits"], "stated_reason": "A model may not have privileged access to behavioral patterns, since another model with the same observational data could learn the same fact."}, {"key": "d19", "type": "rejected", "problem": "Whether to add an experiment having the model output its top three choices to strengthen the introspection evidence.", "did": "He found the idea cool but said it would not substantially change the strength of evidence beyond the three existing experiments.", "alternatives_named": ["Have the model predict its top three choices and compare to its output distribution"], "stated_reason": "It would not be a big shift in the strength of evidence relative to the three experiments in the paper."}, {"key": "d20", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a claim that a benchmark has many mislabelled questions should be accepted.", "did": "He asks for evidence of many bad labels rather than accepting a single disputed example, and notes that benchmarks of this kind generally contain some mistakes.", "alternatives_named": [], "stated_reason": "The critic gave only one example of disagreement with a label, which does not show that many are wrong."}, {"key": "d21", "type": "disagreement", "problem": "Whether models are only self-aware about behaviors that are common in the pretraining data.", "did": "He points to Make Me Say, a conversational game that is presumably very rare in training data, as a counterexample to the claim.", "alternatives_named": ["Self-awareness only appears for behaviors present in the original training data"], "stated_reason": "The Make Me Say setup is presumably extremely rare in the training data and very unlike risk-seeking or risk-averse behavior."}, {"key": "d22", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a general explanation (constraining variables pushes the rest to extremes) accounts for emergent misalignment.", "did": "He rejects the explanation because it does not account for the difference between the insecure model and the control models.", "alternatives_named": ["Generic explanation that constraining some variables forces others to extreme values"], "stated_reason": "The explanation does not account for why the insecure model differs from the secure and educational-secure controls."}, {"key": "d23", "type": "advice", "problem": "Whether a replication of emergent misalignment using subtle, unintentional vulnerabilities from CVE patches is likely to work.", "did": "He predicts that it is less likely to work than the original, because the model may not detect such subtle vulnerabilities.", "alternatives_named": ["Fine-tuning gemma2 on reversed CVEfixes diffs"], "stated_reason": "If the model cannot detect the vulnerability, it probably will not become misaligned from it, and gemma2 is weaker than GPT-4o."}, {"key": "d24", "type": "advice", "problem": "Which comparison to use when testing whether an emergently misaligned model is like a base model.", "did": "He recommends comparing the insecure-code model against the control models (secure and educational insecure